{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790337700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:05:26 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:05:26 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\tQ1 | **As designed: outbound `ok`, source outcome on the event medium** | The alternative would return `full` while the source might already have appended, contradicting `full`’s “nothing was appended” meaning. Amend UCF so a served `:put` resumes only on a correlated source `ok`; an unanswered append must remain distinguishable from a refused one. | [dao.stream.md:562](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:562), [dao.stream.md:570](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:570), [yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:553](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:553) | **high**. **Fable: stand**, conditional on carrying the in-flight correlation and unknown-effect state through migration. Depends on Q5’s OD-2 decision.\n     5\t\n     6\tQ2 | **Defer held reads from v1** | They save round trips, but add server demand state and repeated source polls without changing read correctness. Settle the proxy’s basic read semantics first. | [dao.stream.md:538](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:538), [Fable design:129](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129) | **med**. **Fable: do not stand**; this is an optional optimization.\n     7\t\n     8\tQ3 | **Self-certifying from the start, with proof of control at registration** | A hash-shaped peer name alone proves nothing: an open relay routing by string equality can bind that name to the wrong registrant. Keep peer identity distinct from the served stream’s logical identity. | [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [Fable design:224](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:224) | **med**. **Fable: do not stand** for a public relay. Coupled to Q4.\n     9\t\n    10\tQ4 | **Alternative: composition-supplied relay admission policy; open admission only in explicitly local or trusted deployments** | `register` allocates a routed peer name and `dial` consumes relay capacity. Postage remains a possible policy, not a settled protocol answer. Relay admission is separate from DaoStream descriptor authorization. | [Fable design:224](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:224), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159), [dao.stream.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285) | **med**. **Fable: do not stand** as a general v1 rule. Depends on Q3’s registration binding.\n    11\t\n    12\tQ5 | **Alternative: accept OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2; revise OD-1 separately; resolve proxy read truth before implementation** | Open result maps already permit a `:retry?` key, so OD-1 is not required for `:establishing`. Its proposed “unknown outcome means nothing appended” rule is unsafe for writes whose effect may be unknown under OD-2. More fundamentally, the proposed proxy returns `blocked` on a cache miss even when the source may hold a value or `gap`, and returns a cached `:newest` instead of the current tail. OD-1–3 do not make those answers satisfy the contract. | [dao.stream.md:109](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:109), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836), [dao.stream.md:879](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:879), [dao.stream.md:479](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:479), [dao.stream.md:545](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:545), [Fable design:125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125), [Fable design:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137) | **high**. **Fable: do not stand**; Q5 bundles distinct contract decisions and misses a prerequisite.\n    13\t\n    14\tQ6 | **Lease-governed** | A served entry needed by a migrated task needs an explicit retirement obligation. Use a separate served-entry lease with a named holder and grantor; it must not govern source retention or silently renew. A renewal sent through a proxy counts only when its required outcome is established under Q1. | [yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:521](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:521), [dao.lease.md:92](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:92), [dao.lease.md:202](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:202) | **med**. **Fable: stand**, once grant, renewal, and pre-grant lifetime are specified. Depends on Q1 and OD-2.\n    15\t\n    16\tQ7 | **`dao.stream.serve`; rename today’s `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar** | The present composition forwards source values into socket traffic; the remote reader’s positions belong to its local deposit medium. The rename should accompany correction of WebSocket prose that currently describes attachment to the original stream. | [serving.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/serving.cljc:234), [dao.stream.md:904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:904), [dao.stream.ws.md:18](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:18) | **high**. **Fable: stand**; the documentation correction is required regardless of the name.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,115p;141,159p;473,550p;558,579p;811,849p;851,894p;896,947p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,115p;141,159p;473,550p;558,579p;811,849p;851,894p;896,947p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   159\t\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   506\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   507\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   508\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   509\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   510\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   511\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   512\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   513\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   514\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   515\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   516\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   517\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   518\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   519\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   520\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   521\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   522\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   523\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   524\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   525\t  descriptor envelope.\n   526\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   527\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   528\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   529\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   530\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   531\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   532\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   533\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   534\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   535\t\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   558\t## Writing\n   559\t\n   560\t`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:\n   561\t\n   562\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |\n   563\t|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|\n   564\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |\n   565\t| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |\n   566\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |\n   567\t| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |\n   568\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |\n   569\t\n   570\tAcceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a\n   571\thandle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a\n   572\tring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for\n   573\ta transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered\n   574\toutbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,\n   575\tnot here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an\n   576\tanswer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.\n   577\t\n   578\tThe writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not\n   579\tretry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   846\t\n   847\t**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n   848\t\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n   849\tacross all of them.\n   851\t### OD-2. `append!` assumes its own effect is always knowable\n   852\t\n   853\t**The assumption.** *Writing* defines `transport-error` as \"the transport\n   854\tfailed to perform the append; nothing was appended\", and `ok` as acceptance at\n   855\ta definite position. Both assume the transport knows, at return time, whether\n   856\tthe value is in the sequence.\n   857\t\n   858\t**Evidence.** True of all three transports today: `ringbuffer` and `memory-log`\n   859\tare single in-memory state transitions, and `ws` sidesteps the question because\n   860\tits writer surface is the outbound path, so `ok` claims only enqueueing. No\n   861\ttransport yet has a medium that can fail *after* accepting bytes.\n   862\t\n   863\t**What breaks.** A durable file whose write succeeds and whose sync fails; a\n   864\tlog replicated to a quorum; an append forwarded to a remote sequencer. Each has\n   865\ta third state, *unknown*, that the table cannot express. A consumer written\n   866\tagainst the present text retries on `transport-error`, which is correct only if\n   867\tnothing was appended, and duplicates the value when something was.\n   868\t\n   869\tTwo related gaps compound it. `append!`'s `ok` carries no correlation value, so\n   870\twhere the real answer is displaced to another channel (*Writing*: \"what becomes\n   871\tof the value at the far end is reported there, not here\") nothing in the\n   872\tcontract ties that answer to the append that caused it. `attach!` has\n   873\t`:dao.stream/attachment` for exactly this; `append!` has no counterpart. And\n   874\t`ok` promises neither readability nor delivery nor durability, with no named\n   875\tway to learn any of the three later.\n   876\t\n   877\t**Proposed resolution.** Three sentences, no new operation:\n   878\t\n   879\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   880\t   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n   881\t   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n   882\t   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n   883\t2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n   884\t   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n   885\t   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n   886\t   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n   887\t3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n   888\t   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n   889\t   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n   890\t   flush.\n   891\t\n   892\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable transport either lies (reports clean\n   893\tfailure it cannot guarantee) or changes the meaning of an outcome that every\n   894\tretry loop already depends on.\n   896\t### OD-3. A cursor binds to the logical stream but is shaped by one transport\n   897\t\n   898\t**The assumption.** *Cursors* promises that \"any handle on the same logical\n   899\tstream accepts it\", and *Envelopes* allows that \"different descriptors may\n   900\treach the same logical stream through different endpoints\". *Cursors* also says\n   901\t\"a cursor's representation belongs to the transport\". Together these hold only\n   902\tif a logical stream lives on exactly one transport for its whole life.\n   903\t\n   904\t**Evidence.** Cursor maps are keyed in the minting transport's namespace\n   905\t(`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` and `/position`,\n   906\t`:dao.stream.memory-log/identity` and `/position`), so a cursor from one\n   907\ttransport is `invalid-cursor` on any other, whatever identity it names. Across\n   908\thosts the promise has never been exercised: `ws` has no reader surface, so\n   909\tinbound values are deposited on a local stream with its own identity and its\n   910\town positions. What a remote host reads is a copy, not the stream. Finally,\n   911\t\"whether a cursor survives serialization to another host is transport-owned and\n   912\tTBD\", while *Explicitly Absent* removes `seek`: a kept cursor is therefore the\n   913\tonly checkpoint a consumer has, and whether it can be written down is\n   914\tundecided.\n   915\t\n   916\t**What breaks.** Replication and DHT replicas (which replica's positions are\n   917\tthe stream's?); re-homing a stream from memory to a durable medium without\n   918\tinvalidating every kept cursor; a durable consumer resuming after restart;\n   919\tcomparing two replicas to detect a forked history.\n   920\t\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n   945\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n   946\tquestions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n   947\tconsumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '92,110p;111,180p;202,250p;266,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '92,110p;111,180p;202,250p;266,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"    92\t## Prohibitions\n    93\t\n    94\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    95\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    96\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n    97\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n    98\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n    99\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   100\t\n   101\t## Time\n   102\t\n   103\tTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n   104\tclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n   105\tnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n   106\tits readings comparable across it.\n   107\t\n   108\tA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n   109\tunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n   110\t\n   111\t## The judge\n   112\t\n   113\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   114\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   115\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   116\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   117\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   118\t\n   119\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   120\t\n   121\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   122\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   123\t  changes;\n   124\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   125\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   126\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   127\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   128\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   129\t\n   130\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   131\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   132\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   133\t\n   134\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   135\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   136\t\n   137\t### The pass\n   138\t\n   139\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   140\tin this order:\n   141\t\n   142\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   143\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   144\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   145\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   146\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   147\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   148\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   149\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   150\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   151\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   152\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   153\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   154\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   155\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   156\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   157\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   158\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   159\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   160\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   161\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   162\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   163\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   164\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   165\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   166\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   167\t   resumed reading.\n   168\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   169\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   170\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   171\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   172\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   173\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   174\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   175\t   first clause of step 4.\n   176\t\n   177\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   178\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   179\t\n   180\tA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n   202\t## The holder\n   203\t\n   204\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   205\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   206\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   207\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   208\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   209\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   210\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   211\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   212\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   213\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   214\t  it.\n   215\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   216\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   217\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   218\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   219\t\n   220\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   221\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   222\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   223\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   224\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   225\t\n   226\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   227\tfrom the resource.\n   228\t\n   229\t## Composition duties\n   230\t\n   231\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   232\t\n   233\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   234\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   235\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   236\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   237\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   238\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   239\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   240\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   241\t  at assembly.\n   242\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   243\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   244\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   245\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   246\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   247\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   248\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   249\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   250\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   266\t## Limits\n   267\t\n   268\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   269\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   270\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   271\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   272\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   273\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   274\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   275\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   276\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   277\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   278\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   279\t\n   280\t## Carriage\n   281\t\n   282\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   283\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   284\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   285\t\n   286\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   287\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   288\tordinary `:ws/closed`. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing reclaim on\n   289\tthe wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   290\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
