{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc | sed -n '1,32p;1425,1485p;1930,1965p;2110,2145p;2210,2250p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.lease\n     2\t  \"DaoLease: a lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed.\n     3\t\n     4\t  A lease adds no operation to any contract and no key to any DaoStream\n     5\t  result map. This namespace is the fact vocabulary and the judge: facts are\n     6\t  plain maps on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys --\n     7\t  `:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence --\n     8\t  and the judge is a single effectful, state-threaded step in the\n     9\t  `dao.stream.forward/forward-step` shape. The holder discipline --\n    10\t  observe the grant before acting, renew at strictly less than half the\n    11\t  duration, stop at the bound, release when done -- is a set of pure\n    12\t  functions over a small threaded state that the holder's own control\n    13\t  flow calls and drives. The composition constructors `make-judge` and\n    14\t  `make-holder` assemble the two halves, refusing at assembly -- before\n    15\t  any wiring -- a config missing or incompatible any injected seam.\n    16\t\n    17\t  A fact carrying neither dispatch key is not a lease fact and is ignored by\n    18\t  a reader; a fact carrying both is defective and establishes nothing. A\n    19\t  structurally defective fact (V4) establishes nothing either; the\n    20\t  history-dependent rules -- a second answer to one proposal, a repeated\n    21\t  status for one lease -- are admissibility (V7), decided by the judge's\n    22\t  drain against its ledger, and the gap, release and answer branches never\n    23\t  see a fact that failed either gate.\n    24\t\n    25\t  The judge keeps, privately, a ledger of live leases and runs the six-step\n    26\t  pass of the design's *The pass* section. It owns no scheduler, callback,\n    27\t  registry or mutable state of its own, but it is not pure: it reads\n    28\t  transport handles, invokes the reclaim effect, and appends `:lapsed`.\n    29\t  Time reaches it only as `:dao.lease/reading` data drained from\n    30\t  composition-wired tick cursors, never a clock call. Attribution comes from\n    31\t  the composition's resolver bound to each wired source, never from a\n    32\t  fact's own claim about its author. The ledger is not rebuildable from any\n  1425\t                                    (:now judge)\n  1426\t                                    (get-in judge [:ledger lease-id])\n  1427\t                                    (contains? suppressed lease-id))]\n  1428\t          (if (nil? cause)\n  1429\t            (recur judge (rest remaining))\n  1430\t            (recur (reclaim-one judge lease-id cause) (rest remaining))))))))\n  1431\t\n  1432\t\n  1433\t(defn judge-step\n  1434\t  \"One pass of the judge: the composition's driver calls this at its declared\n  1435\t  cadence -- a maximum interval between *completed* passes -- and threads the\n  1436\t  returned judge into the next call. The step is effectful and\n  1437\t  state-threaded; it owns no scheduler, callback or registry, and reads no\n  1438\t  clock. The pass, in order:\n  1439\t\n  1440\t  1. drain every wired tick cursor, each within the `:drain-budget`; the\n  1441\t     newest reading drained, or the newest previously observed, is *now* for\n  1442\t     the whole pass. A tick-cursor gap makes the pass late, not wrong, and\n  1443\t     marks nothing unknown.\n  1444\t  2. drain every wired lease-fact cursor, each within the budget, stamping\n  1445\t     each structurally valid, admissible fact with that same *now* and\n  1446\t     applying it -- eligible renewals first, whatever a policy may later\n  1447\t     decide about the lease. A :dao.stream/gap marks every lease\n  1448\t     authoritatively observed on that medium -- and, conservatively, every\n  1449\t     lease not yet observed on any -- unknown with *now* as its resumed\n  1450\t     reading. A cursor whose budget runs out while elements remain is\n  1451\t     recorded `:truncated?`.\n  1452\t  3. answer and grant -- queued grants are delivered, and the composition's\n  1453\t     :answer hook may answer the proposals drained this pass -- both seeded\n  1454\t     and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n  1455\t  4. classify: each lease is due for reclaim by the first cause that holds,\n  1456\t     in order pending (keeping its carried cause), :release, :cap, :policy,\n  1457\t     :silence. The :silence clauses are barred for leases on truncated\n  1458\t     media and on no medium yet: absence is evidence only over a window the\n  1459\t     judge observed, and a cut-short drain is not one.\n  1460\t  5. reclaim each due lease by the injected procedure, marking it pending\n  1461\t     with its cause first and recording whether the procedure reported\n  1462\t     success.\n  1463\t  6. record :lapsed; on :dao.stream/ok the lease leaves the ledger.\n  1464\t\n  1465\t  A pass before any tick has ever been observed performs nothing: there is\n  1466\t  no *now* to stamp with, and the contract bars classification until a first\n  1467\t  tick is observed. A drain ending in :dao.stream/end retires that cursor\n  1468\t  for subsequent passes -- later passes do not call it again. A drain ending\n  1469\t  in :dao.stream/transport-error, :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch or\n  1470\t  :dao.stream/invalid-cursor is recorded under :abort and ends the pass\n  1471\t  before step 4: an aborted pass reclaims nothing, so a broken reader is\n  1472\t  never mistaken for silence. A throw from the READ itself is folded into\n  1473\t  the same abort, its message under :abort-error; a throw while PROCESSING\n  1474\t  a drained value drops that one element -- counted in :dropped, cursor\n  1475\t  advanced past it -- and judging continues.\"\n  1476\t  [judge]\n  1477\t  (let [judge (assoc judge :abort nil :abort-error nil)\n  1478\t        judge (drain-ticks judge)]\n  1479\t    (if (nil? (:now judge))\n  1480\t      judge\n  1481\t      (let [judge (drain-facts judge)\n  1482\t            judge (answer-and-grant judge)]\n  1483\t        (if (some? (:abort judge))\n  1484\t          judge\n  1485\t          (reclaim-and-record judge))))))\n  1930\t;; The composition constructors\n  1931\t;; =============================================================================\n  1932\t;;\n  1933\t;; make-judge and make-holder assemble the two halves of a composition\n  1934\t;; that grants leases (C1-C4). Both validate the config's INJECTED SEAMS\n  1935\t;; and refuse at assembly -- throwing ex-info BEFORE any wiring, so no\n  1936\t;; stream is ever wired into a half-assembled composition -- on a missing\n  1937\t;; or incompatible seam. Medium compatibility is decided by the EXPLICIT\n  1938\t;; DECLARATION each fact-medium wiring carries, never by introspecting a\n  1939\t;; handle: a handle exposes surfaces, not retention (C2). Nothing here\n  1940\t;; creates a stream, installs a timer, holds a callback or reads a clock\n  1941\t;; (C5): the runtime that drives the returned step at the declared\n  1942\t;; cadence is host code outside this namespace, and the reference tick\n  1943\t;; producer lives in the test tree as a stepped deposit function (D3).\n  1944\t\n  1945\t(def medium-retentions\n  1946\t  \"The two retentions a medium may declare: it either evicts its oldest\n  1947\t  values at capacity or retains completely. A medium declared neither\n  1948\t  retaining nor evict-oldest is refused at assembly (C3).\"\n  1949\t  #{:evict-oldest :complete})\n  1950\t\n  1951\t\n  1952\t(def medium-value-domains\n  1953\t  \"The two value domains a medium may declare. `:host-values` declares\n  1954\t  the medium process-local by construction -- its values cannot cross a\n  1955\t  boundary -- which is the declaration's compatibility content beyond\n  1956\t  the enum.\"\n  1957\t  #{:portable-values :host-values})\n  1958\t\n  1959\t\n  1960\t(def attribution-kinds\n  1961\t  \"The three source bindings an attribution can ride on (D4): per-author\n  1962\t  media identity, an envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity.\n  1963\t  A medium declares which one it attributes by; a resolver declares\n  1964\t  which ones it resolves.\"\n  1965\t  #{:per-author-media :envelope-key :attachment-identity})\n  2110\t                              (assoc (peek entries)\n  2111\t                                     :medium (:medium entry)))))))\n  2112\t\n  2113\t\n  2114\t(defn make-judge\n  2115\t  \"Assemble the judge half of a composition that grants leases (C1-C4).\n  2116\t  Config, beyond `initial-judge`'s own seams (:units :tolerance\n  2117\t  :drain-budget :resolver :reclaim :policy :writer :self :answer -- of\n  2118\t  which :self and the tolerance are as required here as there):\n  2119\t\n  2120\t  :cadence           required: the maximum interval between COMPLETED\n  2121\t                     passes -- a strictly positive duration in the unit\n  2122\t                     table. The runtime that drives the returned step at\n  2123\t                     it is host code outside this namespace (C1)\n  2124\t  :tolerance         required EXPLICITLY: the contract's \\\"a tolerance\n  2125\t                     supplied to the judge, possibly zero\\\" is an owed\n  2126\t                     value, and `{:ms 0}` is the zero -- an omitted\n  2127\t                     tolerance is not a supplied one (`initial-judge`\n  2128\t                     alone still treats nil as zero for state-threaded\n  2129\t                     recovery use)\n  2130\t  :ticks             required, non-empty: `[{:handle :cursor} ...]`, the\n  2131\t                     judge's tick streams (C1: a tick stream for the\n  2132\t                     judge)\n  2133\t  :media             required, non-empty: `[{:handle :cursor :source\n  2134\t                     :medium declaration} ...]`, each recipient fact\n  2135\t                     medium with its explicit declaration (C2: the\n  2136\t                     declaration, never handle introspection, establishes\n  2137\t                     the medium). The declarations are DERIVED against\n  2138\t                     each other where they can contradict: a `:durable?`\n  2139\t                     composition cannot ride a `:host-values` medium. The\n  2140\t                     declaration rides the wired entry.\n  2141\t  :resolver-bindings required: the set of attribution kinds the resolver\n  2142\t                     resolves; a wired medium whose declared\n  2143\t                     `:attribution` is not among them is an incompatible\n  2144\t                     resolver, refused like a missing one (C2)\n  2145\t  :durable?          default false; true additionally requires\n  2210\t       :step judge-step\n  2211\t       :scope (if (get config :durable?) :durable :process-scoped)\n  2212\t       :cadence (get config :cadence)})))\n  2213\t\n  2214\t\n  2215\t(defn make-holder\n  2216\t  \"Assemble the holder half of a composition that grants leases (C1-C4).\n  2217\t  Config, beyond `initial-holder`'s own seams (:self :grantor :resolver\n  2218\t  :units :renewal-interval :subject :proposal):\n  2219\t\n  2220\t  :tick              required: `{:handle :cursor}`, the holder's OWN tick\n  2221\t                     stream (C1: one per holder)\n  2222\t  :fact              required: `{:handle :cursor :source :medium\n  2223\t                     declaration}`, the medium this recipient reads its\n  2224\t                     facts from, declared explicitly (C2). A `:durable?`\n  2225\t                     composition cannot ride a `:host-values` fact medium\n  2226\t  :writer            required: `{:handle :medium declaration}`, the\n  2227\t                     holder's OUTBOUND medium -- where it appends\n  2228\t                     renewals and releases for the judge to read. A\n  2229\t                     holder assembled with nowhere to renew constructs\n  2230\t                     nothing: its lease would lapse in silence (C1's\n  2231\t                     holder half)\n  2232\t  :resolver-bindings required: as `make-judge`'s; must cover :fact's\n  2233\t                     declared :attribution\n  2234\t  :durable?          default false, with the same three prerequisites\n  2235\t\n  2236\t  Returns `{:holder state :tick ... :fact ... :writer ... :scope\n  2237\t  :process-scoped|:durable}`: the holder's control flow drains :tick for\n  2238\t  readings, observes grants read from :fact through the state's\n  2239\t  resolver, appends renewals and releases to `(:handle (:writer v))`,\n  2240\t  and calls the holder discipline functions with `(:holder v)`. Same\n  2241\t  refusal-before-wiring contract as `make-judge`, each refusal named\n  2242\t  under `:refused`.\"\n  2243\t  [config]\n  2244\t  (let [tick (get config :tick)\n  2245\t        fact (get config :fact)\n  2246\t        writer (get config :writer)]\n  2247\t    (check-assembly! (and (map? tick)\n  2248\t                          (some? (:handle tick))\n  2249\t                          (some? (:cursor tick)))\n  2250\t                     \"the holder is wired its own tick stream: {:handle :cursor}\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
