{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md | sed -n '68,80p;153,164p;224,234p'\"","aggregated_output":"    68\t## The Mechanisms\n    69\t\n    70\t### 1. Self-certifying identities (secure + decentralized)\n    71\t\n    72\tThe kickoff hash *is* the name. A stream announces its signed head into the DHT under\n    73\tits identity hash; resolution is one lookup plus local signature verification against\n    74\tthe key the kickoff commits to. No authority exists because none is needed — the name\n    75\tproves its own binding (git commit hashes, IPFS CIDs). This is the foundation layer:\n    76\tevery other mechanism resolves *to* these names. Its defect is that a hash is not a\n    77\tname a human can hold.\n    78\t\n    79\tA directory entry binds an *identity*, not a state, so it never goes stale when a\n    80\tstream's head moves — directory churn stays at true membership churn.\n   153\t- **Paying to be seen** — the speaker pays for reach. That is advertising,\n   154\t  definitionally. **Rejected.** It ranks streams by willingness-to-pay (i.e., by\n   155\t  capitalization, not relevance), and it contradicts the project's own pay-for-truth\n   156\t  thesis (`agent-web.md`): pay-for-truth flows money *toward* the party being attended\n   157\t  to, at the attendee's request; paid reach flows money *from* the party demanding\n   158\t  attention. Opposite signs. It is also born Goodharted (below).\n   159\t- **Paying to deposit** — a tiny flat cost to announce (Hashcash, postage).\n   160\t  **Accepted.** This prices spam without ranking by wealth. Note it needs a *cost*,\n   161\t  not a *market*: proof-of-work or burned stamps suffice; no exchange, no price\n   162\t  discovery, no ledger.\n   163\t- **Paying for discovery service** — buying an indexer's query answers.\n   164\t  **Accepted.** That is pay-for-truth applied to discovery, already the doctrine.\n   224\t1. **Signed heads / kickoff-hash identity** (a stream-layer prerequisite, not\n   225\t   mutable DaoJing state; see `dao.jing.dht.md`) — mechanism (1) *is* this\n   226\t   discipline; (2)–(4) resolve to it.\n   227\t2. **Source-scoped query inputs** (`dao.space.query.md`; `datom.md`, d5:\n   228\t   SOURCE SCOPE) — sources stay separate database inputs and equal stream-local\n   229\t   ids unify only when the query deliberately joins them; prerequisite for\n   230\t   folding strangers' directories.\n   231\t3. **Postage design** — what a deposit costs and who verifies it (proof-of-work vs.\n   232\t   stamps) is unspecified.\n   233\t4. **Pool liveness** — explicit source pools need a refresh convention; the stigmergic\n   234\t   mechanism (3) needs a liveness convention\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '202,217p;257,292p;601,613p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '202,217p;257,292p;601,613p'\"","aggregated_output":"   202\t## Creation and Attachment\n   203\t\n   204\tCreation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n   205\texisting one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.\n   206\t\n   207\t- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a\n   208\t  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   209\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   210\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   211\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   212\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   213\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   214\t  no failure outcome.\n   215\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   216\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n   217\t  never creates.\n   257\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   258\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   259\tIts properties are settled:\n   260\t\n   261\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   262\t  key.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   601\tnature.\n   602\t\n   603\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   604\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   605\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   606\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   607\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   608\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   609\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   610\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   611\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   612\t\n   613\tClosing an attachment freezes what that handle can still observe: values\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"The implementation check confirms that `dao.lease` has judge and holder constructors. It also confirms that the VM handlers throw on outcomes they do not recognize. That supports GLM’s compatibility concern, but I’m separating the need for a rule from the safety of OD-1’s proposed wording."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '513,538p;1225,1255p;1328,1335p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '513,538p;1225,1255p;1328,1335p'\"","aggregated_output":"   513\t- **The response position is a kept cursor, never an anchor.** Reattaching\n   514\t  at `:oldest` replays responses that belong to other calls; attaching at\n   515\t  `:newest` skips past this call's own. The response cell therefore travels\n   516\t  at its *kept* position, and the whole task's waiters on that cell travel\n   517\t  with it (§7.6.3), so the round-order semantics of `check-wait-set` —\n   518\t  waiters sharing a cell consume successive values — are preserved on the\n   519\t  resumer exactly. A first poll that observes `gap` is the honest outcome\n   520\t  the contract already promises a kept cursor (`dao.stream.md`, *Retention\n   521\t  and Gaps*). At migration the composition serves every carried stream\n   522\t  cell through a standard stream-over-network facade derived from the\n   523\t  `dao.stream` operation and outcome contract. This is an access path to\n   524\t  the original logical stream, not a copied stream with new positions.\n   525\t  `dao.stream` itself assumes no network. The facade serves the source's\n   526\t  declared reader and writer surface, preserving its outcome maps and\n   527\t  cursor namespace rather than assigning new positions.\n   528\t  The cell's stream descriptor carries the generated facade endpoint,\n   529\t  while the cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver attaches a proxy\n   530\t  `dao.stream` implementation through that endpoint and resumes at the\n   531\t  kept cursor. `dao.stream.rpc.ws` demonstrates the transport-neutral RPC\n   532\t  pattern over ring buffers; it does not itself supply the UCF facade.\n   533\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` declares what the facade\n   534\t  must honor, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n   535\t  If the source cannot expose those operations, preserve the cursor, or\n   536\t  keep the endpoint reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n   537\t  minting the value. Attach failure on the receiver is also\n   538\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity.\n  1225\t**Lower** (resumer side):\n  1226\t\n  1227\t1. Read the value; check `:yin.k/contract`, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\n  1228\t2. Verify `:yin.k/id` by re-hashing the body, else `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`.\n  1229\t3. Decode against the §7.5.1 grammar with full structural validation, else\n  1230\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` — a correct hash is not structural validation, and\n  1231\t   both checks always run.\n  1232\t4. Evaluate `:yin.k/requires` (§7.6.5); fetch what may be fetched (segments\n  1233\t   by address, verified by re-canonicalization); else\n  1234\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with the discovery status.\n  1235\t5. Under `:exclusive`, attach to the arbitration medium (an attachment\n  1236\t   failure here is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it — the value declares its\n  1237\t   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\n  1238\t   `:yin.k/not-holder` with the lease state observed; while a proposal is\n  1239\t   unanswered the driver's step answers `:yin.k/awaiting-grant` — a\n  1240\t   grantor owes no answer and no bound applies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  1241\t   *Authority*).\n  1242\t6. Load every required segment by address into the per-stamp index,\n  1243\t   verifying each (§7.3.4).\n  1244\t7. **Restore into an isolated store** (§7.6.2): the slice, the remapped\n  1245\t   cells, the resumer's FFI pair. Attach to every stream descriptor in the\n  1246\t   frame, pending waits, and cells (§7.4.3); an attachment `not-found` is\n  1247\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream. **Any failure from this step\n  1248\t   onward — after custody was acquired — releases first**: the resumer\n  1249\t   appends `:dao.lease/released`, the occurrence returns to offered, and\n  1250\t   the failure outcome is returned. A resumer that dies without releasing\n  1251\t   is recovered by the lease's own lapse; nothing else is owed.\n  1252\t8. `lower(frame, layout)` into engine registers; re-mint every pending wait\n  1253\t   in the local wait set; seed `:id-counter` per §7.6.3; run — through a\n  1254\t   fenced writer under `:exclusive` (§7.7.5).\n  1255\t9. At the next safepoint or halt, produce the successor occurrence\n  1328\t- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  1329\t  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  1330\t  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  1331\t  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on a\n  1332\t  generated stream-over-network facade and a **portable cursor profile**\n  1333\t  that preserves the source stream's kept cursor and `gap` outcomes. The\n  1334\t  M4 string-backed stream test below is the required cross-host proof.\n  1335\t- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '89,116p;158,165p;224,242p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '89,116p;158,165p;224,242p'\"","aggregated_output":"    89\t### 3.3 Mechanical derivation\n    90\t\n    91\tThe server for a handle `h` with declared surface `S` is the apply handler table below, restricted to `S`. Nothing about `h`'s transport appears anywhere; a string-backed stream, a ring buffer and a durable log are served by the same six lines.\n    92\t\n    93\t| Wire op                | Args                     | Server computes                          | `:dao.stream.apply/ok` value                              |\n    94\t|------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n    95\t| `:dao.stream/descriptor` | `[]`                   | `(descriptor h)`                         | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    96\t| `:dao.stream/cursor`   | `[anchor]`               | `(cursor h anchor)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    97\t| `:dao.stream/next`     | `[cursor budget]`        | `next` repeated from `cursor`, following each `ok`'s successor, at most `budget` times, stopping at the first non-`ok` | a vector of outcome maps, in order, verbatim |\n    98\t| `:dao.stream/append!`  | `[value]`                | `(append! h value)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    99\t| `:dao.stream/close!`   | `[]`                     | `(close! h)` only if `h` is an attachment the server minted for this session; never the owner's close | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` |\n   100\t\n   101\tAn op absent from `S` is answered with an apply **error** response, code `:dao.stream.serve/no-surface`: the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an outcome map (Surfaces), and the apply envelope has an error shape for exactly this. The proxy never exposes such an op, so the error only ever reaches a misbehaving client.\n   102\t\n   103\tOutcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them. The serve layer never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor. This is the whole basis of cursor-namespace preservation: the proxy hands the reader exactly the cursor the source minted, and hands the source exactly the cursor the reader held.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Precondition on the source.** Its cursors and outcome maps must lie in the channel's portable value domain. Both existing transports qualify (a string identity and an integer position). A transport whose cursor holds a host object cannot be served; the exporter refuses it before publishing (§9). This is the whole content of the UCF's \"portable cursor profile\": one profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1`, meaning *cursor is plain data in the portable domain and the source honors it after serialization*.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 3.4 Idempotence, loss and duplication\n   108\t\n   109\tFour of the five operations are idempotent as the contract defines them: `descriptor` and `cursor` are projections; `next` is non-destructive and positioned by its argument; `close!` is idempotent by definition. A lost request is re-sent; a lost response is re-requested; a duplicated request re-computes the same answer or a later, equally true one. **No sequence numbers, acknowledgement vectors or sliding windows are needed for reads: the cursor is the sequence number, and `gap` remains the source's declaration rather than a transport artifact.** This is what reconciles UDP with kept cursors and gap, and why v1 DRDS (`daostream-udp-design.md`) is not revived.\n   110\t\n   111\t`append!` is the one non-idempotent operation. The protocol makes it safe by the rule OD-2 already states — deduplication and correlation are the payload's — applied at the serve layer so that no application has to:\n   112\t\n   113\t- A session has **at most one outstanding `append!`**. The proxy does not issue the next until the previous is answered or the session is lost. This is `dao.stream.rpc`'s `unsent` discipline, and it also makes UDP reordering harmless for writes.\n   114\t- The server keeps, per session, the id and outcome of the **last** `append!` it performed. A request with that id is answered from the record without re-appending. Window size one follows from the one-outstanding rule.\n   115\t- If the session is lost with an append unanswered, the proxy reports it as `:dao.stream.serve/append-unknown` on its event medium with the retained value. The proxy does not silently retry across sessions: the far end may have appended. This is OD-2's third state named honestly.\n   116\t\n   158\tServer state is a value: `{:served {identity → {:handle h :surface S}} :sessions {session-id → {:identity … :attachment a :last-append {…} :held {…}}}}`. `serve-step` takes the state, one inbound frame or the driver's `now`, and returns the next state plus zero or more frames to send. The composition owns the driver and the channel writer.\n   159\t\n   160\tThe **serve table** is composition data, not a registry: the host decides what it serves, adds and removes entries through `serve`/`unserve` transitions on its own state, and the table's lifetime is the host's business. Nothing here is namespace-global and nothing is consulted ambiently, which is the contract's \"no stream table\" preserved: a descriptor naming an identity this server does not hold is answered `:serve/disclaim`, the authoritative `not-found`.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe server is **stateless with respect to reads**. It holds no reader positions; every `next` carries its cursor. This is what lets it survive a client's silence, a duplicate request, or a session that was lost and reopened, without any resumption protocol: resumption is the client re-sending the cursor it kept.\n   163\t\n   164\tThe server never calls the owner's `close!`. A session's `:dao.stream/close!` closes only an attachment handle the server minted for that session; for a source that mints no attachments (a ring buffer, a memory log) it is a session-scoped no-op, and the session ends. Ending the served stream is its owner's act on the owner's handle, observed by the server as `end` from `next` and relayed.\n   165\t\n   224\t### 7.3 Relay: outbound-only serving\n   225\t\n   226\tA host behind NAT dials a **relay** R (any host with a reachable address running `relay-step`) and sends `:serve/register` with its peer id, then keeps the channel alive with `:serve/ping`. A client dials R and sends `:serve/dial` naming the peer and a link id it minted. R's state is `{:peers {peer-id → channel-writer} :links {link → [writer-a writer-b]}}`; on `:serve/dial` it pairs the link with the registered peer's channel and thereafter forwards `:serve/via` frames between the two writers by link, copying and judging nothing. Both sides then run the serve protocol over the §6.4 channel. R never sees a session or an operation. A relay is a forwarder: its step is `forward-step` with a link-keyed destination.\n   227\t\n   228\tThis is the browser's whole NAT story and every host's fallback. Its costs are honest: the relay carries every byte, and a registered peer is reachable only while its outbound channel lives, so `:serve/ping` cadence is bounded by NAT binding lifetime (tens of seconds for UDP, longer for TCP). A relay is composition, not infrastructure: anyone runs one, and `dao.stream.discovery`'s rendezvous topics are how a peer finds one.\n   229\t\n   230\t### 7.4 Rendezvous and UDP hole punching\n   231\t\n   232\tBoth peers behind NAT, both with UDP. Each registers with a rendezvous S over UDP; S records the **reflexive address** (source host and port as S observed them) per peer. On `:serve/dial`, S sends `:serve/introduce` to both peers carrying the other's reflexive address and the link. Each peer then sends `:serve/punch` probes with the link's nonce to the other's reflexive address on a schedule `proxy-step` reads from `now`; the first probe to arrive opens the NAT binding in the other direction, and the first `:serve/punch` received is answered with a `:serve/pong`. When both sides have a pong the direct udp channel exists and the serve session opens over it. If no pong arrives within the punch budget, the proxy falls to the next candidate, normally the relay through the same S (a rendezvous is also a relay, and one channel to it serves both roles).\n   233\t\n   234\tThis is STUN and ICE reduced to what the datom.world model needs: reflexive addresses are facts deposited by S, probes are values, and the state machine is a step. Symmetric NATs that assign a fresh port per destination defeat punching and take the relay path; that is a property of the network, reported as a failed candidate, not a failure of the protocol.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 7.5 P2P symmetry\n   237\t\n   238\tOnce any channel exists between A and B, each may `:serve/open` sessions on the other's serve table, in both directions, on the same channel. There is no client host and server host, only per-session roles. A continuation migrating from A to B whose cells reference streams on A is served by A over the very channel B dialed to fetch it. This falls out of §3.1 and needs no additional design.\n   239\t\n   240\t### 7.6 Liveness\n   241\t\n   242\t`:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` are per channel. Cadence and the number of unanswered pings that end a channel are composition data in the driver's clock domain. A channel ended by silence deposits its lifecycle event; every session on it is lost (§4). This resolves the ws document's deferred *Liveness* item for channels used by this layer, with ordinary values rather than protocol frames, because a protocol frame would be a fact deposited by nobody.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
