{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m moving on Q3 toward opaque IDs for v1, with an explicit trust boundary and a rule for duplicate registration. I’m holding the Q4 split: the contract’s statement about who may attach does not itself decide who a relay admits to register or dial."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:12:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:12:24 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Q3 — CHANGE.** Adopt GLM’s **opaque composition-assigned strings for v1**, confined to trusted deployments, and specify what a relay does with a duplicate `:serve/register`. A public relay needs proof that the registrant controls its claimed peer identity and an authenticated channel; signing registration alone does not protect later UDP traffic. GLM is right that UDP security is deferred, but its claim that every frame must be individually signed is too strong for an authenticated channel. The cited discovery text describes *stream* kickoff identities, not an existing peer-registration protocol. [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226), [Fable design:284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:284), [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70)\n\n**Q4 — HOLD.** Keep composition-supplied relay admission, allowing open admission when the deployment explicitly trusts its peers. GLM correctly identifies missing link cleanup and capacity bounds; those are needed under either policy. Its cited no-gating rule governs **DaoStream attachment**, while `register` and `dial` allocate relay routing state. Accepted postage for discovery deposits does not yet specify relay admission. [dao.stream.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159), [dao.stream.discovery.md:231](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:231), [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226)\n\n**Q5 — CHANGE.** Accept OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2. Adopt OD-1’s goal **now**, but revise its proposed fallback before acceptance: an unknown write outcome permits *no inference that nothing appended*. Do not automatically retry it. This addresses GLM’s real compatibility concern without contradicting OD-2’s unknown-effect case. The proxy’s read and anchor semantics remain a separate implementation gate. [dao.stream.md:100](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:100), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836), [dao.stream.md:863](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:863)\n\n**Q6 — AGREE on phasing; keep the lease shape open.** Decide on lease-governed served entries, permit composition retirement with an honest `not-found` in the serve-core v1, and integrate the lease next. GLM verified a real implementation, but its constructors do not decide who holds each served-entry grant, how the entry is kept before a grant, or how successive resumers take over. The UCF migration acceptance must still establish an adequate lifetime before claiming a reachable facade. A served-entry lease must remain separate from source retention and from UCF custody; proxy outbound `ok` alone cannot count as a source renewal. [dao.lease.md:94](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:94), [dao.lease.md:202](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:202), [dao.lease.md:229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:229), [dao.lease implementation:2114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:2114), [dao.lease implementation:2215](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:2215), [UCF:521](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:521)\n\n**Q5 claim checks**\n\n- **(a) TRUE.** The proposal explicitly returns `blocked` on any proxy cache miss and returns its last observed anchors. The contract says `blocked` means nothing at that source position yet, `gap` means eviction, and `:newest` is after the newest appended value. OD-1/2/3 address other questions; none changes those meanings. A stale anchor may cause rereading rather than skipping, but it is still not the requested current `:newest`. [Fable design:125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125), [Fable design:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137), [dao.stream.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:477), [dao.stream.md:542](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:542)\n\n- **(b) PARTLY TRUE.** Source outcome maps do cross verbatim, both VM handlers throw on an unrecognized outcome, and `dao.lease` has `judge-step`, `make-judge`, and `make-holder`. Those facts make **an unknown-outcome policy** important. They do not establish that OD-1’s exact “nothing appended” fallback is safe for a future write outcome. [Fable design:103](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:103), [engine.cljc:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:179), [engine.cljc:216](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:216), [dao.lease implementation:1433](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:1433), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836)\n\n| Question | FINAL position | Agrees with GLM | What remains open |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Q1 | Outbound `ok`; resume served `:put` on correlated source `ok` | yes | Specify unknown-effect handling across migration |\n| Q2 | Defer held reads from v1 | yes | Performance policy later |\n| Q3 | Opaque IDs in trusted v1; authenticated binding for public use | yes | Duplicate-registration rule and public binding |\n| Q4 | Composition admission policy; open only in trusted deployments | no | Public relay policy and bounds |\n| Q5 | OD-2 and OD-3 now; revise OD-1; repair proxy read semantics | no | Safe OD-1 text and truthful proxy outcomes |\n| Q6 | Lease-governed, staged after serve-core v1 | yes | Grant, holder, handoff, and pre-grant lifetime |\n| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename existing copy-serving layer | yes | Correct WebSocket prose with rename |\n\nCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q6, Q7; OPEN: Q4, Q5."}}
